# hashi — environment template. Copy to .env and fill in.
# Every value here is FAKE or blank. Never commit a real one; never print .env.

# --- control plane ----------------------------------------------------------
# Encrypts node keys and provider credentials at rest. The app refuses to start
# without it. Generate with: python -c "import secrets;print(secrets.token_urlsafe(48))"
HASHI_SECRET_KEY=replace-me-with-48-random-bytes

# Public hostname of the delivery endpoint. Must be reachable from inside Russia,
# or users cannot refresh configs exactly when a node has just been blocked.
#
# A NAME with a real DNS record, never a bare address. A public CA cannot issue for an
# IP, so Caddy falls back to its own CA and every stock client refuses the certificate —
# the subscription then fails for every user while nothing on the host looks wrong.
HASHI_PUBLIC_HOST=hashi.example.com

# The certificate every DELIVERY host is served under, and its private key, BY PATH
# INSIDE THE CONTROL CONTAINER — M27.6. The files themselves go in `deploy/delivery/` on
# this host and these two values are where the control plane opens them from, which is
# not the same path; the paragraph below says it again, because a value here read as a
# path on the host is how the feature was left unreachable once already. A delivery host
# is a node that answers its own users' subscription refreshes, so that a refresh works
# at the moment this control plane is behind a closed border. Which hosts those are is
# derived and has no setting.
#
# BOTH BLANK IS A SUPPORTED MODE and is the default: no node serves refreshes of its own
# and every user fetches from this control plane, exactly as before. Set both only when
# a node other than this box is to answer.
#
# ONE WILDCARD FOR THE FLEET (`*.example.com`), never one per host: a certificate is
# published in a Certificate Transparency log within minutes of being issued, so one per
# host would put every entry node's name into a feed anyone can subscribe to, as it is
# created.
#
# **Issue it for a zone that carries nothing else, and never the one above.** The private
# key is pushed to every delivery host — the machines most likely to be seized — and
# whoever holds it can answer for ANY name the certificate covers, until it expires.
#
# **THE PATHS ARE INSIDE THE CONTROL CONTAINER, and the files go in `deploy/delivery/`
# on this host.** That directory is bind-mounted read-only at `/etc/hashi/delivery`
# (deploy/docker-compose.yml), so the two values below are the only ones that work
# without editing compose — a path somewhere else on the host is a path the control plane
# cannot open, and it fails the same way as leaving these blank: no node serves refreshes
# and the Nodes screen says which of the two it is.
#
# **AND READABLE BY AN ACCOUNT YOU DID NOT CHOOSE.** The control plane runs as uid 999
# (`hashi`, created by `useradd --system` in deploy/docker/control.Dockerfile), and the
# files are owned by you. At the 0600 a private key normally carries, that account cannot
# open it: the control plane refuses the pair, the Nodes screen says so and names the
# path, and no host serves refreshes. Give it access — `chown 999:999` both files, or
# `chgrp 999` and `chmod 0640` — rather than `chmod 0644`, which works and also hands the
# key to every other account on this box.
#
# Renewal is replacing the two files in `deploy/delivery/` — no restart, no migration:
# the pair is read on every apply and never cached, and a host is handed the current one
# by the next apply that reaches it. A node never talks to a certificate authority.
# **Check the mode again every time**, because this is where it comes back: `cp -p`
# preserves the issuer's 0600, and a certbot deploy hook writes its own.
#
#   HASHI_DELIVERY_CERTIFICATE_PATH=/etc/hashi/delivery/fullchain.pem
#   HASHI_DELIVERY_KEY_PATH=/etc/hashi/delivery/privkey.pem
HASHI_DELIVERY_CERTIFICATE_PATH=
HASHI_DELIVERY_KEY_PATH=

# --- reaching the panel (M7) -------------------------------------------------
# The console is NOT on the public hostname above. Caddy serves two sites: the
# public one answers `/sub/*` and 404s everything else, and a private one answers
# `/panel/*`, `/api/*` and `/static/*` on the port below — published only on the
# management tunnel's address and on loopback. See docs/deploy/runbook.md,
# "Reaching the panel".
#
# What the owner's device asks for over the tunnel. **An address is the expected
# value**, because that is what a WireGuard tunnel hands you — and because a phone
# cannot be given a hosts entry, so a name is not available on the device most likely
# to be reached for in an emergency.
#
# Caddy issues this certificate from its own CA, so the value never reaches a public
# CA's logs. It is also the Caddyfile's `default_sni`: a TLS client sends no SNI when
# the host is an IP literal, so this is the certificate served to a client that named
# no host at all — which, over this tunnel, is every client.
#
# A NAME works too and costs a hosts entry on every device that must reach the console.
# It buys one thing: because default_sni is global, a no-SNI connection to the PUBLIC
# port is answered with this certificate as well, so whatever is written here is
# disclosed to anyone who asks that way. An address is private and unroutable, which is
# why it is still the default.
HASHI_PRIVATE_HOST=10.0.0.1

# Where Caddy listens for that site. Inside this project's block (54100–54299) and
# not a service default. Changing it changes both the container's listener and the
# published mapping — they are the same variable on purpose, so they cannot drift.
HASHI_PRIVATE_PORT=54143

# Which address on THIS host the private port is published on — the management
# tunnel's own address, and nothing else. Read only by deploy/docker-compose.tunnel.yml,
# which `make up-panel` loads on top of deploy/docker-compose.public.yml; `make up` and
# `make up-dev` never publish it at all.
#
# Never 127.0.0.1: the fallback mapping in public.yml already claims loopback for this
# port, and docker refuses the second bind with "port is already allocated".
#
# And never an address this host does not hold, which is what a machine restored from a
# bucket has — its tunnel's keys were on the box that was lost. Leave this UNSET there
# and publish with `make up-panel-over-ssh`, which is the same publication without the
# one mapping that needs a tunnel: the console then answers on loopback only and you
# reach it with `ssh -L`. Set it and move to `make up-panel` once the tunnel is up.
HASHI_PRIVATE_BIND_ADDR=10.0.0.1

# --- the management tunnel (optional; scripts/management-tunnel.sh) ----------
# Read by the tunnel generator that `make panel-bootstrap` runs. Absent means no
# tunnel is generated and the panel is reachable only over the SSH fallback — a
# real mode, and the bootstrap says so rather than pretending otherwise.
#
# Everything here is per-deployment. Nothing is defaulted: a shared address range
# collides with a hop, and a shared port is a port the fleet cannot move away from.
#
# THE VALUES BELOW ARE THIS TEMPLATE'S, AND THE BOOTSTRAP KNOWS IT. Left as they are,
# `make panel-bootstrap` generates NO tunnel and says so: generating one from them would
# write a tunnel here and print a private key for each of the example devices below,
# pointing at the example endpoint — fabricated credentials for a host that is not this
# one, on the terminal of somebody who asked for none of them. Fill the block in with
# this deployment's own values and run it again to get a tunnel.
#
# This host's end of the tunnel, with a prefix.
HASHI_MGMT_ADDRESS=10.0.0.1/24
# The UDP port the tunnel listens on. Open it in the host firewall.
HASHI_MGMT_PORT=51821
# Where the owner's devices dial this host. An address or a name, no port.
HASHI_MGMT_ENDPOINT=hashi.example.com
# Explicit and required, exactly as the transport requires it — never defaulted.
HASHI_MGMT_MTU=1420
# The owner's devices, `name=address`, comma separated. At least two, because an
# owner has a laptop and a phone: this tunnel takes N peers by construction and is
# deliberately NOT a node_transports row, which carries one peer address and would
# therefore serve one device. Every bootstrap run converges the tunnel to this list —
# delete a name to take that device's access away. ADDRESS, PORT and MTU above are
# inside every device config already handed out, so changing one later is refused
# until the old tunnel is retired (docs/deploy/runbook.md, "Starting the tunnel over").
HASHI_MGMT_PEERS=laptop=10.0.0.2/32,phone=10.0.0.3/32

# --- the operator ------------------------------------------------------------
# There is exactly one operator (charter: "one operator, one fleet"), so these come
# from the environment rather than from a users table — a table would imply the
# public signup the charter names as a non-goal.
#
# NEVER put the password itself here. Generate the hash, never type it:
#
#   make hash-password             on the host itself, with nothing installed but the
#                                  container runtime. It builds the control image and
#                                  runs the command below inside it.
#   python -m control.cli hash-password    on a machine that already has this project
#                                  set up (`make setup`, Python 3.14).
#
# Both prompt, so the plaintext stays out of your shell history too, and both print the
# whole line to paste here. The first exists because the second could not be run on the
# machine that needs it most: a host rented an hour ago has the system's own Python — on
# Ubuntu 22.04 that is 3.10 — no venv, and no way to make one, which turned "fill in four
# values" into "have a second working computer" during a rebuild.
#
# Paste the line it prints EXACTLY as printed, doubled $$ and all, and do not double
# them again. docker compose interpolates the values it reads from this file and turns
# each $$ back into one $; a lone $ there would start a variable reference and the rest
# of that field would arrive empty — which is a control plane its own operator cannot
# log into, with a correct-looking hash sitting right here. The control plane now
# refuses to start on a hash that is not one, so the mistake is loud either way.
HASHI_OPERATOR_USERNAME=owner
# The placeholder names `make hash-password` and not the other route, because this is the
# value an operator meets on a host where the other route cannot run: the placeholder is
# read literally on a bare machine at 3am, and until M28.12 it named the one command that
# needs a second working computer. `scripts/panel-bootstrap.sh` reads this exact line out
# of this file to refuse an env file nobody filled in, so the two cannot drift.
HASHI_OPERATOR_PASSWORD_HASH=run-make-hash-password-and-paste-the-output

# --- database ---------------------------------------------------------------
POSTGRES_USER=hashi
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=replace-me
POSTGRES_DB=hashi

# HASHI_DATABASE_URL is NOT set here on purpose: deploy/docker-compose.yml composes
# it from the three values above, so the app and the database cannot be pointed at
# different places. Set it by hand only when running the app outside compose, e.g.
# against a loopback-published dev database:
#   HASHI_DATABASE_URL=postgresql+asyncpg://hashi:replace-me@127.0.0.1:54132/hashi

# --- how often the control plane dials on its own (M26 fix round 5, M31.2) ---
# Every this-many seconds, the control plane probes each node that is IN SERVICE and
# carries a probe finding, so a verdict clears itself once the host is serving again
# instead of waiting for somebody to press probe. A probe only reads: it writes no
# file on a node and changes no state. Blank means the default, 60.
#
# HASHI_FINDING_REMEASURE_INTERVAL=0 switches it off, and nothing dials a node unless
# you press apply or probe. That is the setting for a control plane you have just
# restored and have not looked at yet: `make restore-from-bucket` holds it at 0 by
# itself until `make up-panel` (or `make up-panel-over-ssh`) brings back the value
# written here — set 0 here to keep it off after that too.
HASHI_FINDING_REMEASURE_INTERVAL=

# --- sites that only open from Russia (M34) ----------------------------------
# An entry node with "direct sites" switched on (Nodes screen) opens the sites on the
# Routing screen's lists from its own Russian address; everything else goes through the
# hop. The lists are fetched by this control plane — never by a node — once a night in
# the backup's hour (HASHI_BACKUP_AT / HASHI_BACKUP_TZ, below), and the entries whose
# config changed are applied then. Applying restarts an entry's proxy: anyone connected
# through it is dropped for a second or two. Every value here may stay blank.
#
# How often, in seconds, the control plane asks whether tonight's run is due (blank:
# 900). 0 switches the nightly run off; "fetch now" on the Routing screen still works.
HASHI_ROUTING_CHECK_INTERVAL=
# A fetched list is HELD for you, with its diff, instead of applied when it grows or
# shrinks by more than this fraction in one fetch (blank: 0.5), holds more entries than
# HASHI_ROUTING_MAX_ENTRIES (blank: 20000 — also the most one entry's config may carry
# across all lists), or has any line that is not a site or an address range. A download
# past HASHI_ROUTING_MAX_BYTES (blank: 4000000) or HASHI_ROUTING_FETCH_TIMEOUT seconds
# (blank: 30) is a failed fetch, and the list in force stays.
HASHI_ROUTING_MAX_CHANGE=
HASHI_ROUTING_MAX_ENTRIES=
HASHI_ROUTING_MAX_BYTES=
HASHI_ROUTING_FETCH_TIMEOUT=
# The sources the Routing screen offers to add: `name|https-url|on-or-off` entries
# separated by `;`. Blank: the four in deploy/docker-compose.yml — the narrow
# itdoginfo Russia/outside list (switched on when added) and three broader roscomvpn
# lists (added switched off). Set it to replace that list.
HASHI_ROUTING_SUGGESTED_SOURCES=

# --- the owner's Telegram bot (M37, M39) ------------------------------------
# One Telegram account — yours — can add a user by sending the bot `/adduser <name>`;
# every other account gets silence. Both blank, the default, means there is no bot:
# nothing polls and nothing is logged. Set BOTH or neither — one without the other stops
# the bot at boot, with a sentence naming the pair.
#
# Since M39 the bot runs as its OWN container (`telegram-bot`) beside the control plane,
# tied to it by the shared database — the control plane no longer polls. It is off unless
# you ask for it: fill the two values below, then bring the stack up with the profile on,
# e.g. `make up PROFILES=telegram-bot` (or `make up-panel PROFILES=telegram-bot` on a
# deployed host). A plain `make up` leaves the bot off, exactly as before.
#
# The token is the one BotFather gives you and it is a credential: whoever holds it reads
# your commands and writes to you as the bot. It belongs here and nowhere else — not in
# a chat, a screenshot or a ticket — and if it leaks, revoke it at BotFather and paste
# the new one. The id is your own NUMERIC Telegram user id, never a username: a username
# can be changed and reassigned, the number cannot.
HASHI_TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
HASHI_TELEGRAM_OWNER_ID=
# Where the Bot API is dialled. Blank: Telegram's own, https://api.telegram.org. Set it
# only to a relay you run yourself, for a box from which Telegram's address does not
# answer; the e2e stand points it at a fake.
HASHI_TELEGRAM_API_URL=
#
# THE BOT LEAVES THROUGH A LEAF ABROAD — M40, and it is mandatory in this profile. The
# `telegram-bot-egress` sidecar runs an AmneziaWG client to a leaf, and the bot shares its
# network namespace: Telegram and the SSH to nodes both exit there, only the shared database
# stays direct. There is no variable for it — the sidecar reads a client config you place at
# `deploy/egress/awg0.conf` (see deploy/egress/README.md for the shape and why). A Russian
# host cannot reach api.telegram.org directly, which is the whole reason this exists; with no
# config there, the sidecar never comes up and the bot waits rather than polling in the clear.

# --- caddy ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Email used for ACME registration. Read only once a publishing command
# (`make up-panel`, `make up-panel-over-ssh`) switches public issuance on — the
# stack `make panel-bootstrap` and a restore bring up publishes nothing and asks no
# CA for anything (M31.1, deploy/docker-compose.public.yml).
CADDY_ACME_EMAIL=owner@example.com

# --- offsite backup (M28) ----------------------------------------------------
# Everything the control plane generates, uploaded once a day to an object store
# that is not this host. The store is addressed by the S3 protocol, NOT by a
# provider name: CLAUDE.md forbids hardcoding a provider exactly as it forbids
# hardcoding a node, and for the same reason — a provider you cannot rotate away
# from is a provider you are stuck with on the day you need to leave it.
#
# Absent or HASHI_BACKUP_ENABLED=0 means the artifact is written and stays on this
# host. That is a real mode and the stack says so rather than pretending it is
# covered. It does NOT switch the schedule off: a daily backup that never leaves
# the machine is still a backup, and `make backup-schedule` keeps taking it. What
# stops the schedule is `make backup-unschedule`.
#
# It is read from THIS FILE and nowhere else: `HASHI_BACKUP_ENABLED=1 make backup`
# on the command line does not switch the upload on. Two things read this value —
# the uploader and the thing that writes the backup record — and only a file both
# of them can see keeps them saying the same thing about the same run.
HASHI_BACKUP_ENABLED=0

# The endpoint, region and bucket of the object store. All three are per-
# deployment and none is defaulted, because a default here is a silent upload to
# somebody else's idea of the right country.
#
# The endpoint is the store's OWN address, with a scheme; the bucket goes in the
# PATH — https://<endpoint>/<bucket>/<prefix><file>. Do not fold the bucket into
# the hostname: that form asks the store to answer for every bucket name as a
# subdomain, which is one provider's DNS convention rather than the protocol, and
# the point of this block is that the store is replaceable. Use https — over http
# the key id below and the name of your bucket travel in the clear, and the upload
# refuses unless the endpoint resolves to a private network.
#
# A RESTORE READS THESE FOUR TOO, and that is why the values here are not harmless
# placeholders on a machine being rebuilt. `make restore-from-bucket` takes
# HASHI_RESTORE_S3_ENDPOINT, _REGION, _BUCKET and _PREFIX from its own environment and
# falls back to the four below when they are unset — which is right on a host that was
# taking backups, and on a host rebuilt from this template means it goes looking for the
# example store on the line under this one. Either fill these four in, or name the
# HASHI_RESTORE_S3_ four in the command; it prints which of the two each value came from
# before it asks the store anything. The block at the bottom of this file has the
# credentials that go with them.
HASHI_BACKUP_S3_ENDPOINT=https://storage.example.net
HASHI_BACKUP_S3_REGION=example-region-1
HASHI_BACKUP_S3_BUCKET=example-bucket-name
# Optional key prefix inside the bucket. Blank puts objects at the root.
HASHI_BACKUP_S3_PREFIX=control/

# The credential this host uploads with. It must be WRITE-ONLY — PutObject and
# nothing else: no DeleteObject, no ListBucket, no GetObject.
#
# This is not belt-and-braces. An attacker who holds this box holds this key, and
# a key that can delete turns a seizure into a total loss of the backup history
# as well as the machine. But a key that can only write can still overwrite: it
# chooses the object's name, so it can write over a name that is already there.
# What makes write-only stick is BUCKET VERSIONING, which is required, plus
# object lock where the store has it and a lifecycle rule whose expiry you
# chose. That is settled in docs/deploy/runbook.md, "The retention this host's
# own key cannot reach", which also says how to check it is on — you cannot
# check it with this key.
HASHI_BACKUP_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
HASHI_BACKUP_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# What a failed upload leaves you holding: the artifact, here, intact — and
# `make backup` exiting 3 rather than 0, because a backup that never left the
# machine is not an offsite backup and must not be reported as one. Send the same
# file when the store is reachable again with `make backup-upload FROM=<path>`;
# nothing is dumped a second time.

# The age public key the dump is encrypted TO, before it leaves this host.
#
# Decided by the owner 2026-09-22: the whole dump is encrypted, not just the
# columns the application encrypts. Without this, a reader of the bucket who
# never obtains HASHI_SECRET_KEY still gets every node's real address — including
# the leaf addresses a user is never shown — plus how many users there are
# and when each one arrived.
#
# A KEYPAIR, deliberately, not a passphrase: this host holds only the public
# half, so it can encrypt a backup and cannot read one back. The private identity
# never touches this machine. It is what you carry, beside HASHI_SECRET_KEY, and
# the two of them are what a restore costs.
#
# Leave blank for now — the keypair is generated during M28 and you will be given
# the private half to keep; the public half is what goes here.
HASHI_BACKUP_AGE_RECIPIENT=

# When the daily run happens, and in which zone. The zone is explicit because a
# host's clock is UTC and its owner is not, and "04:00" that silently means
# something else is only ever discovered during a restore.
#
# HASHI_BACKUP_AT is HH:MM, 24-hour, read as a wall-clock time in the zone below.
# HASHI_BACKUP_TZ is an IANA zone NAME — `Europe/Paris`, `Asia/Tokyo`, `Etc/UTC` —
# and never an offset like `+03:00`. A name follows the rule; a number is a
# decision taken once about a rule that changes, by season and by decree, and the
# day it changes nothing anywhere says so.
#
# What happens when this is wrong, which matters more than what happens when it is
# right — in every case the backup still happens, and the fault is recorded rather
# than costing you a day:
#
#   unset/empty      the host's own clock is used and the record says `unset`.
#                    No zone is defaulted to: there is no correct country to guess.
#   not a real zone  the same, recorded as `unknown`. It is checked against this
#                    host's zone database rather than trusted, because
#                    `TZ=Nowhere/Useful date` does not fail — it quietly answers
#                    in UTC, which is exactly the silent skew this variable exists
#                    to prevent.
#   not HH:MM        04:00 is used and the record says `malformed`.
#
# The schedule is installed with `make backup-schedule` — one cron entry, no root,
# nothing new on the host. It fires every 15 minutes and carries no hour of its
# own, so the run starts at the first tick at or after the time above, and — this
# is the part that matters — A DAY IS NEVER SILENTLY SKIPPED. If the box was off,
# asleep or being rebuilt at 04:00, the backup is taken when it comes back, and
# every day that had none is written down by date.
#
# Where it is written down: `backups/state/last-run.json` on this host, which is
# also the only place the answer can live — the credential above may only write,
# so the bucket cannot be asked what is in it. `make backup-status` reads it.
HASHI_BACKUP_AT=04:00
HASHI_BACKUP_TZ=Etc/UTC

# NOT SET HERE, on purpose: the credentials a RESTORE needs.
#
# Restoring reads from the bucket, so it needs a credential that may ListBucket
# and GetObject — which is exactly what this host must not hold, or the
# write-only key above bought nothing. All three of these are carried by you and
# handed to one command at the moment you run it:
#
#   HASHI_RESTORE_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID, HASHI_RESTORE_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY
#   HASHI_RESTORE_AGE_IDENTITY  (path to the private half of the key above)
#
#   HASHI_RESTORE_AGE_IDENTITY=/path/to/identity \
#   HASHI_RESTORE_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=... HASHI_RESTORE_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=... \
#   make restore-from-bucket
#
# Never write them into this file on the control plane's own host — and putting
# them here is not a working shortcut: `make restore-from-bucket` reads the two
# S3 names from its own environment and refuses when they are absent from it,
# whatever this file says. See docs/deploy/runbook.md, "Coming back from the
# bucket, on a machine that has never run this".
#
# HASHI_RESTORE_S3_ENDPOINT, _REGION, _BUCKET and _PREFIX exist too and are not
# secrets. Leave them unset and the restore uses the four HASHI_BACKUP_S3_ values
# above, which is where they already are on a host that was taking backups; set
# them to read a bucket other than the one this host writes to.

# --- the agent fleet ---------------------------------------------------------
# Moved out: GH_TOKEN, GIT_USER_NAME, GIT_USER_EMAIL, REPO_URL, MAX_CONCURRENT
# and REASONING_EFFORT now live in deploy/warden/.env(.example), the fleet's
# own env file — a fleet building this app should not need this file's
# secrets, and this app should not need the fleet's. See deploy/warden/README.md.

# --- published ports --------------------------------------------------------
# This project's block is 54100–54299, bound to 127.0.0.1 only. The offsets are
# readable on purpose: +00 app HTTP, +32 Postgres (5432), +90 gRPC. The e2e
# stack takes the next hundred so both can run at the same time.
#
# Never publish a service's default (5432, 6379, 8000, 3000…): the host already
# runs something — Kaizen's own dev overlay publishes 127.0.0.1:5432. Inside the
# compose network services keep their standard ports, which never collide.
HOST_PORT_BASE=54100
DEV_API_PORT=54100
DEV_DB_PORT=54132
E2E_API_PORT=54200
E2E_DB_PORT=54232
# The e2e stand's fake Telegram, its injection side only (M37.4): where a scenario puts
# a message in front of the bot and reads what the bot sent. The control plane reaches
# the fake inside the compose network and never through this port. +80, nothing's default.
E2E_TELEGRAM_PORT=54280
